@inject IStringLocalizer<ErrorMessages> Errors

@* One refusal, rendered under the input that produced it (NFR-4, FR-83).

   DtFailureBanner is still the place a refusal with no field goes, and the place a refusal on a
   field this screen does not render goes. This is the other half: on a long form, being told that
   something is wrong at the top of the page and having to hunt for the box is the failure the
   banner alone could not fix.

   The field is a plain string attribute and not a `For="@@(() => ...)"` lambda, deliberately. A
   `=>` inside an attribute closes the tag as far as NFR-14's source scan is concerned, which is the
   documented reason this product has never had per-field messages - so the one construct that
   caused it is the one construct this component must not reintroduce.

   No label: the input's own <label> is directly above, so naming the field again here would be the
   stutter DtFailureBanner's Label() exists to avoid. The claimed list is resolved into a property
   rather than called inside the @@foreach, for the reason the banner resolves its label into a
   local - a call in markup reads as rendered text to that same scan. *@

@foreach (var failure in Claimed)
{
    <div class="form-text text-danger" role="alert">@Errors[failure.MessageKey]</div>
}

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// The CLR property name a validator would put on the failure - <c>Model</c>, <c>Pickup</c>,
    /// <c>PackageWeightKg</c>. Matched against the root segment <c>FailureKeys</c> already
    /// normalized to, so an input called <c>Pickup</c> claims a rule that fired on
    /// <c>Pickup.Latitude</c>.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? Field { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// The refusals the screen collected, bound unchanged from the same list the banner reads.
    /// Empty renders nothing, which is why a form can bind this unconditionally.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    public IReadOnlyList<ScreenFailure> Failures { get; set; } = [];

    /// <summary>The refusals this field owns.</summary>
    private IReadOnlyList<ScreenFailure> Claimed => FailureKeys.ClaimedBy(Failures, Field);
}
